{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011ChLHLke7a5JvsbRUGshU4","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"`trigsimp` now turns your first example into `sin(k)`, and the second into `sin(k)/k`. It does not return `sinc(k)` for the second one, which was only a \"would be nice\" in the issue.\n\n`test_trigsimp.py` and `test_fu.py` pass, apart from `test_issue_6811_fail`. That test is marked XFAIL and was already failing on a clean checkout. I stopped a wider run over `sympy/simplify` and `sympy/functions/elementary` after it timed out, so those directories are untested.\n\nI made two changes in `sympy/simplify/trigsimp.py`:\n- **`exptrigsimp`:** when an expression contains `exp`, it now also tries the `cos` rewrite followed by `expand(mul=True)`. It keeps whichever form has the fewest operations. The `cos` rewrite alone wasn't enough; the expansion is what collapses it to `sin(k)`.\n- **`trigsimp` (default method):** the default `'matching'` method now runs `exptrigsimp` before `futrig`, but only when the expression contains both `exp` and `I`.\n\nI added the `exp` and `I` condition because running `exptrigsimp` unconditionally broke two tests. It rewrote real exponentials like `exp(2)` as `sinh(2) + cosh(2)`, and it changed a `sin(1)` result in `test_trigsimp_issues`."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":570,"cache_read_input_tokens":30140,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":570},"output_tokens":468,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"94b69b66-3c4b-4217-9d06-a02a22512e7b","uuid":"00df8eb0-bd78-4bcc-9cbb-62fabda17760","timestamp":"2026-11-23T17:49:59.499Z","request_id":"req_011ChLHLkVS2Ymznu9JK4gXP"}
